  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. Its first run came back `ok`. I didn't open the dashboard to look at the panels, only confirmed it was created.\n\n**Data view fields:** `kibana_sample_data_logs` holds web-server access logs. The main fields are:\n- `timestamp` and `utc_time`\n- `response.keyword` (the HTTP status code, stored as a string)\n- `bytes`\n- `clientip`, `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `extension.keyword`, `machine.os.keyword`, `host.keyword`, `url.keyword` and `request.keyword`\n\n**Dashboard \"EFF-4e615d1d Web logs overview\"** (id `a3611aac-253b-40dc-b44e-14d4fa6355c7`) has four panels. All four use ES|QL queries on `kibana_sample_data_logs`:\n- Total requests\n- 5xx responses\n- Average bytes\n- Requests over time\n\n**Rule \"EFF-4e615d1d 5xx spike\"** (id `f91a57d5-8531-4366-85f2-64253614a4cc`) is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It looks at the last 5 minutes and fires when the 5xx count is above 10. The rule has no actions attached, so it records alerts but doesn't send notifications.\n\n**ES|QL rule workaround:** ES|QL rules only accept a threshold of 0 (fire when any row is returned). So the \"more than 10\" check is inside the query: `FROM kibana_sample_data_logs | WHERE response.keyword LIKE \"5*\" | STATS count = COUNT() | WHERE count > 10`.",
